    // Actually, segments [i,j] cover the interval [i,j]. Two segments [i,j] and [k,l] overlap
    // if their interiors intersect, i.e., max(i,k) < min(j,l).
    // The minimum number of layers is the maximum number of segments that are pairwise overlapping,
    // which for intervals is the maximum depth (maximum number of intervals covering any point).

    // Let's compute for each point x from 1 to N-1, how many segments [i,j] have i < x < j?
    // No, segments [i,j] with integer endpoints. The segment [i,j] covers all real points between i and j.
    // Two segments overlap if they share any point other than possibly endpoints.
    // So [0,1] and [1,2] do NOT overlap (they only touch at 1).
    // [0,2] and [1,3] overlap because they share (1,2).

    // The maximum number of segments that can be drawn in one layer is such that no two overlap.
    // This is the interval graph coloring problem. The chromatic number equals the clique number,
    // which is the maximum number of intervals that all overlap at a common point.

    // For a point x (real number), count segments [i,j] with i < x < j.
    // The maximum over all x gives the answer.
    // Since the endpoints are integers, the maximum will occur at some x = k + 0.5 for integer k.
    // At x = k + 0.5, the segments covering it are those with i <= k and j >= k+1, i.e., i < k+0.5 < j.
    // So i <= k and j >= k+1.
    // Number of such segments: i can be 0..k (k+1 choices), j can be k+1..N (N-k choices).
    // So the count is (k+1) * (N-k) for k = 0, 1, ..., N-1.

    int maxLayers = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k < N; k++) {
        int count = (k + 1) * (N - k);
        if (count > maxLayers) {
            maxLayers = count;
        }
    }

    cout << maxLayers << endl;

    return 0;
}
